# Credentials for the filmnotes tooling. Copy to .env and fill in - .env is git-ignored
# and must never be committed.
#
#   cp .env.example .env && $EDITOR .env
#
# The whole repository is bind-mounted into the Docker Sandbox, so a .env *here* is readable
# from inside it. To keep the credentials out of the sandbox, put the file somewhere else and
# point FILMNOTES_ENV_FILE at it (on the host, in your shell profile):
#
#   mkdir -p ~/.config/filmnotes && mv .env ~/.config/filmnotes/env
#   export FILMNOTES_ENV_FILE=~/.config/filmnotes/env
#
# Anything already exported in the shell wins over this file.

# --- Scan-import CLI (tools/scan-import) ------------------------------------------------
# The PocketBase instance the scans are uploaded to.
FILMNOTES_SERVER_URL=https://pb.example.com
# The app user that becomes the owner of the created scan records.
FILMNOTES_EMAIL=me@example.com
# Password of that user. Leave empty to be asked interactively on every import - a flag would
# put it into the process list and the shell history.
FILMNOTES_PASSWORD=

# --- PocketBase server (backend/, only needed where the server actually runs) ------------
# Copy this section to backend/.env next to docker-compose.yml.
#
# The superuser of the admin UI, used once by the bootstrap command in backend/README.md.
PB_ADMIN_EMAIL=
PB_ADMIN_PASSWORD=
# Exactly 32 characters; encrypts the PocketBase settings table (SMTP/S3 credentials) at rest.
# Generate with: openssl rand -hex 16
# Only takes effect once the `command:` block in backend/docker-compose.yml is uncommented.
PB_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# --- Not here on purpose ----------------------------------------------------------------
# The app's own secrets (server password, WordPress application password) are entered in the
# app and stored in the device keychain (expo-secure-store), never in a file. There is no
# build-time environment for them: an Expo bundle ships to a device, and anything baked into
# it is readable by whoever holds the device.
